C = [
("h1", "10. 1 Corinthians 11:2–16 and Its Stated Grounds"),
("p", "Claim: the passage on the head covering gives five stated grounds, and they are not of one kind. Two are from the order God has set and from creation, one is the angels, one is nature and propriety, and one is the practice of the churches. The volume reports each and prescribes no practice of covering. It records that the passage assumes that women pray and prophesy where the church meets."),
("sub", "10.1 The setting"),
("p", "The passage opens with praise: “Now I praise you, brethren, that ye remember me in all things, and keep the ordinances, as I delivered them to you” (1 Corinthians 11:2). The instruction that follows is about praying and prophesying: “Every man praying or prophesying, having his head covered, dishonoureth his head” (1 Corinthians 11:4), and “every woman that prayeth or prophesieth with her head uncovered dishonoureth her head” (1 Corinthians 11:5). The meeting is not named in these verses. It is named in verse 16, where Paul speaks of “the churches of God,” and in the next section, “when ye come together in the church” (1 Corinthians 11:18). Thomas Constable reads the setting as the church at worship, writing that “Paul’s reference to ‘praying or prophesying’ seems to set his instructions in the context of the church at public worship.” The volume reads it so and notes that Constable himself records that others do not."),
("p", "One point stands out that does not depend on the setting. Paul assumes that women pray and prophesy. He does not forbid it in verse 5; he speaks of how it is done. Constable, writing on 1 Corinthians 14, says that “Paul had formerly acknowledged that women could share a word from the Lord in the church meetings (11:4–16).” N. T. Wright draws the same conclusion from the verses and presses it: “What the passage cannot possibly mean is that women had no part in leading public worship, speaking out loud of course as they did so.” The volume agrees with both as a reading of 11:5, and notes that it leaves the question of 14:34 to chapter 11."),
("sub", "10.2 The five grounds"),
("p", "The first ground is an order. “But I would have you know, that the head of every man is Christ; and the head of the woman is the man; and the head of Christ is God” (1 Corinthians 11:3). Three heads are named, and the third is God’s headship over Christ. The word “head” is the same each time. Constable notes that scholars take it as either source or authority and says, “Both meanings are true to reality, so it is difficult to decide what Paul meant here.” Wright thinks a good case can be made that the sense in this verse is source rather than sovereignty. The volume does not decide the sense. It notes that Paul does not define the word in the verses and that the verses that follow reason from origin and purpose, which is the next ground."),
("p", "The second ground is creation. “For a man indeed ought not to cover his head, forasmuch as he is the image and glory of God: but the woman is the glory of the man” (1 Corinthians 11:7). “For the man is not of the woman; but the woman of the man. Neither was the man created for the woman; but the woman for the man” (1 Corinthians 11:8, 9). The reasons are of the first man and the first woman, and they are given as reasons, with “for.” Paul balances them at once: “Nevertheless neither is the man without the woman, neither the woman without the man, in the Lord. For as the woman is of the man, even so is the man also by the woman; but all things of God” (1 Corinthians 11:11, 12)."),
("p", "The third ground is “For this cause ought the woman to have power on her head because of the angels” (1 Corinthians 11:10). Paul does not say what the angels see or why. The volume reports the verse and builds nothing on it. The fourth is nature and propriety: “Judge in yourselves: is it comely that a woman pray unto God uncovered? Doth not even nature itself teach you, that, if a man have long hair, it is a shame unto him? But if a woman have long hair, it is a glory to her: for her hair is given her for a covering” (1 Corinthians 11:13–15). The fifth is custom: “But if any man seem to be contentious, we have no such custom, neither the churches of God” (1 Corinthians 11:16)."),
("sub", "10.3 What the five grounds are and are not"),
("p", "The grounds are not all of one kind. The first two are about God’s order and creation, and Paul states them as facts. The third is a reason Paul gives without explanation. The fourth appeals to the Corinthians’ own sense (“Judge in yourselves”) and to nature, and it speaks of shame and glory. The fifth appeals to what the churches do. Constable counts these as “five arguments for women wearing head-coverings in that culture,” and concludes that Paul “appealed to his readers to understand God’s intention for men and women, and to maintain a custom, not to obey a command of God.” Piper holds a related view, writing that “The Bible is very aware of the role of culture in shaping the symbols of man’s headship and woman’s submission.”"),
("p", "The volume agrees with the two writers in a limited way. Paul’s language in this passage is different from his language in chapter 14. Here he says “ordinances” (11:2) and appeals to what is comely (11:13) and to what the churches do (11:16). There he says “the commandments of the Lord” (14:37). The volume therefore does not say that the passage commands a covering as a rule for the Body. It also does not say it does not. Its report is the grounds, and its observation is that the first two, the headship and creation, are stated as facts and are not tied to Corinth, while the covering itself is argued from nature and from what the churches do. That is why the volume prescribes no practice of covering. It says what Paul grounds on creation and what he grounds on custom, and leaves the reader to see the difference."),
("ctbl", "The five grounds of 1 Corinthians 11:2–16.", [
    ("Ground", "Verses", "What it rests on"),
    ("The order of heads", "11:3", "Christ, the man, the woman, and God over Christ"),
    ("Creation", "11:7–9, 11, 12", "Image and glory; the woman of the man and for the man; in the Lord, neither without the other"),
    ("The angels", "11:10", "Stated without explanation"),
    ("Nature and propriety", "11:13–15", "“Judge in yourselves”; shame and glory; long hair as a covering"),
    ("The churches’ practice", "11:16", "“we have no such custom, neither the churches of God”"),
  ], [0.26, 0.20, 0.54]),
("sub", "10.4 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul assumes that women pray and prophesy and writes about how it is done.",
    "Five grounds are given, of different kinds: an order, creation, the angels, nature and propriety, and the churches’ practice.",
    "The first two are stated as facts and are not tied to Corinth; the covering is argued from the other grounds.",
    "The volume reports the grounds and prescribes no practice of covering.",
  ]),
]
